WEBVTT

00:04.000 --> 00:04.360
All right.

00:04.400 --> 00:09.000
Next we're going to talk about how to use the subquery in the Join clause.

00:09.040 --> 00:14.680
All right so now as we are joining tables in SQL sometimes we have to go and prepare the data before

00:14.720 --> 00:20.040
doing the join to dynamically create a result sets for joining with another table.

00:20.040 --> 00:23.000
So again here we cannot join tables directly.

00:23.000 --> 00:26.480
We have to do a preparation step before doing the joins okay.

00:26.520 --> 00:27.560
Let's have the following task.

00:27.600 --> 00:33.480
And it says show all customer details and find the total orders of each customer.

00:33.520 --> 00:36.120
Now of course in SQL you don't have only one solution.

00:36.120 --> 00:37.640
You have multiple solutions.

00:37.640 --> 00:40.680
But I would like to solve this task using the subquery.

00:40.680 --> 00:43.120
So now if you check the task we have like two parts.

00:43.120 --> 00:45.880
The first part we have to show all the customer details.

00:45.880 --> 00:48.440
And the second part we have like here an aggregation.

00:48.600 --> 00:51.040
Find the total orders of each customer.

00:51.040 --> 00:54.440
So now let's solve those different parts using two different queries.

00:54.480 --> 00:55.960
Let's start with the easiest one.

00:56.000 --> 00:57.360
Show all customer details.

00:57.360 --> 00:59.240
So I think this is very simple.

00:59.440 --> 01:04.270
So select It starts from sales customers.

01:04.550 --> 01:06.350
So let's go and execute it.

01:06.430 --> 01:09.150
So in the output we have all the details about the customers.

01:09.150 --> 01:11.110
And we have solved the first part.

01:11.150 --> 01:11.910
Very simple.

01:11.950 --> 01:14.230
Now let's go and solve the second part.

01:14.270 --> 01:18.270
We have to find the total number of orders of each customer.

01:18.310 --> 01:21.150
That means let me just have a semicolon over here.

01:21.630 --> 01:23.990
We have to go to the table orders.

01:23.990 --> 01:32.270
So let's go and select first the order ID customer ID from the table sales orders like this.

01:32.590 --> 01:35.750
So I will just highlight the second query and execute it.

01:35.790 --> 01:39.510
Now in the output we have ten orders and we have the different customers.

01:39.550 --> 01:43.950
Now in order to find the total orders for each customer, we have to go and use the groupby in order

01:43.950 --> 01:44.550
to do that.

01:44.550 --> 01:45.630
It's very simple.

01:45.630 --> 01:48.630
We're going to go over here and say, so count.

01:48.990 --> 01:50.190
Let's go with the star.

01:50.350 --> 01:58.110
And then we're going to go and group up the data by the customer ID I will go and call this total orders.

01:58.430 --> 02:01.110
So let's go and execute only this part.

02:01.110 --> 02:01.180
parts.

02:01.180 --> 02:05.420
And with that we have for customers and we have the total number of orders.

02:05.420 --> 02:09.100
So with that, we have solved the second part of the task.

02:09.100 --> 02:13.700
So now what I'm going to do, I'm going to go and execute both of those queries using the semicolon

02:13.700 --> 02:17.860
separately like this I will just make this a little bit bigger.

02:17.860 --> 02:19.300
So let's go and execute it.

02:19.340 --> 02:24.820
Now in the output we have the two results, all details about the customers and the total number of

02:24.820 --> 02:26.420
orders for each customer.

02:26.460 --> 02:30.740
So now what we want to do is to go and combine those two results in one.

02:30.740 --> 02:33.860
And in order to do that we can use the joins.

02:33.860 --> 02:36.500
So now we have to think about what is the first query.

02:36.500 --> 02:37.900
What is the second query.

02:38.220 --> 02:43.340
Since the first query returns all the customers that we have in the database, I would like to have

02:43.340 --> 02:44.820
this as the left table.

02:44.820 --> 02:50.620
And since in the second query we have only four customers, I would like to have it then as the right

02:50.660 --> 02:56.580
table and I will go with the left join so that I don't miss any customer, because if I do the inner

02:56.580 --> 02:58.700
join I will lose the customer number five.

02:58.700 --> 03:00.020
So let's go and do that.

03:00.020 --> 03:03.360
So this is the first query in the main query.

03:03.360 --> 03:06.480
So I'm going to call this main query.

03:07.520 --> 03:10.920
And now I'm going to give this as well an alias like the C.

03:10.960 --> 03:16.360
And now we're going to go and join this table from the database together with the results.

03:16.400 --> 03:18.680
The output of this query.

03:18.680 --> 03:22.160
So that means we're going to do it like this left join.

03:22.160 --> 03:24.800
And now we're going to join with a sub query.

03:24.800 --> 03:31.720
So we will have our parentheses I will just put here few spaces so that it's clear it is a subquery.

03:31.720 --> 03:33.360
And we need for this an alias.

03:33.360 --> 03:35.880
So let's go and say for example the oh.

03:36.080 --> 03:40.800
So with that we are joining a table with the results of a subquery.

03:40.840 --> 03:44.360
And now of course what is missing is joining the tables using a key.

03:44.400 --> 03:49.280
Now if you took the two results you can see in both queries we have the customer ID.

03:49.480 --> 03:53.440
That's why we're going to join with the customer ID so on.

03:54.120 --> 04:02.550
Then the customer ID with the customer ID from the subquery Like this.

04:02.550 --> 04:04.030
So we have everything.

04:04.070 --> 04:05.670
Let's go and execute it.

04:05.710 --> 04:09.950
Now, as you can see in the output, we have all the details about the customer.

04:09.950 --> 04:16.030
And as well, together with the total number of orders for each customer, together with the total number

04:16.030 --> 04:17.750
of orders for each customer.

04:17.750 --> 04:20.070
And as you can see, we didn't miss any customers.

04:20.070 --> 04:25.510
So we have all the customers from the database and we can see that Anna doesn't have any orders.

04:25.550 --> 04:27.350
Now you might say, you know what we have here?

04:27.350 --> 04:28.590
The customer ID twice.

04:28.590 --> 04:33.590
So what I'm going to do, I will select all the columns from the customers.

04:33.590 --> 04:38.310
But from the subquery I'm interested only on the total orders.

04:38.510 --> 04:39.630
So like this.

04:39.630 --> 04:40.830
Let's go and execute it.

04:40.830 --> 04:42.950
Let's make this a little bit smaller.

04:42.950 --> 04:44.470
So now the results are really clean.

04:44.470 --> 04:49.790
We have all the details from the customers and as well the total orders of each customer.

04:49.790 --> 04:54.430
And of course as we learned, if you would like to check the results from only the subquery, you go

04:54.430 --> 04:56.150
and highlight it and execute it.

04:56.150 --> 04:59.550
So as you can see you can put the subqueries almost everywhere.

04:59.550 --> 05:02.630
And this is how we use subqueries inside joins.
